coverage={'systems':[U,G,C,N,K],'basis':'Broad malignant tumor families based on public WHO structures and fifth-edition nomenclature; rare morphologic variants consolidated. Major molecular associations checked against primary studies and classification authors’ updates.','merged':['Renal molecularly defined carcinomas remain separate because merging them would obscure defining drivers.','Testicular/ovarian/CNS germinoma-family tumors are one entry with site-specific caveats; other GCT lineages span sites.','Common glandular histotypes are separated where molecularly different; rare site variants are combined with aliases or explicit notes.','Skin melanoma pathways are nested inside one cutaneous melanoma entry; mucosal, conjunctival, uveal and meningeal disease are separately located.','Salivary counterparts cover lacrimal analogues without assuming identical prevalence.','Site-independent sarcomas and lymphomas are covered in their lineage sections; gynecologic ESS/adenosarcoma remain here.'],'excluded':['Benign/borderline and in situ lesions, including clear cell papillary renal cell tumor, ovarian borderline tumors and mature ovarian teratoma.','Most benign testicular and ovarian stromal tumors; variable-behavior families are labelled malignant where applicable.','Metastatic involvement is not repeated as a new primary entity.'],'limitations':['Not an exhaustive current-WHO code audit; selected exceptionally rare site-defined variants are consolidated.','Rare skin, genital and odontogenic carcinomas often lack adequate sequencing cohorts. Empty columns denote an evidence gap, not proven genetic absence.','Several rare-entity associations are based on small series; reported findings are not described as obligatory drivers.','Scope is a fifth-edition foundation and has not been fully reconciled against every sixth-edition volume.']}
Path(__file__).with_name('epithelial.json').write_text(json.dumps({'sources':S,'entities':E,'coverage':coverage},indent=2,ensure_ascii=False), encoding='utf-8');print(len(E))
